#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175736 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a3e455da-2c31-55af-8122-6fa0e5020404" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Surface by Its Polar Equation"}] :question/id #uuid "52199b9c-bb04-5c14-b4ca-909d46c7f039" :question/math-academy-id "e-8808" :question/problem "What surface is defined by the equation $\\theta=3$ in cylindrical polar coordinates?" :question/worked-solution "The position of a point in cylindrical polar coordinates is written as $(r,\\theta,z),$ where \n\n- $r\\geq 0$ and $\\theta\\in [0,2\\pi)$ are the usual plane polar coordinates, and\n\n- $z\\in (-\\infty, \\infty)$ is the usual Cartesian $z$-coordinate.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our equation:\n\n$$\n\\theta=3\n$$\n\nThis equation requires that if we project the vector $\\overrightarrow{OP}$ onto the $xy$-plane for every point $P$ on our surface, the resulting vector makes an angle of $3$ radians with the positive $x$-axis. So, we must have $\\theta=3$ for every point on the surface. On the other hand, $r$ and $z$ can vary arbitrarily in their respective domains. \n\nTherefore, our surface is a half-plane that passes through the $z$-axis, shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038052/assets/2908fb8953f692c596822ecf3fb6454cc5f02f1620f3936d2f5a198d0e9a380d.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175737 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "eae7e5be-e883-5658-84a0-5de5da567887" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Polar Equation of a Given Surface"}] :question/id #uuid "ee821db7-b420-5f37-a54c-a0bbe6cc83be" :question/math-academy-id "e-8809" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038052/assets/1ea30ad7159ada825af44e58f3747f378815e572c0b18d3e8916fd2a6eec58cf.png)\n\n \n\nWhat is the equation of the surface shown above in cylindrical polar coordinates?" :question/worked-solution "The position of a point in cylindrical polar coordinates is written as $(r,\\theta,z),$ where \n\n- $r\\geq 0$ and $\\theta\\in [0,2\\pi)$ are the usual plane polar coordinates, and\n\n- $z\\in (-\\infty, \\infty)$ is the usual Cartesian $z$-coordinate.\n\nThe picture shows a circular cylinder of radius $5$ whose axis of symmetry is the $z$-axis. \n\nWe note the following:\n\n- Since the distance from any point on our surface to the $z$-axis equals $5,$ we must have $r=5$ for every point on the surface.\n\n- On the other hand, $\\theta$ and $z$ can vary arbitrarily in their respective domains.\n\nTherefore, the equation of the surface in cylindrical polar coordinates is $r = 5.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175748 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2f4472e5-68af-5e8c-83cb-ceab2a6dfe1e" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Cones and Half-Cones"}] :question/id #uuid "f948d39d-6d7d-56b8-bb2c-b0a74ac514b7" :question/math-academy-id "e-8893" :question/problem "What surface is defined by the equation $z=4r$ in cylindrical polar coordinates?" :question/worked-solution "The position of a point in cylindrical polar coordinates is written as $(r,\\theta,z),$ where \n\n- $r\\geq 0$ and $\\theta\\in [0,2\\pi)$ are the usual plane polar coordinates, and\n\n- $z\\in (-\\infty, \\infty)$ is the usual Cartesian $z$-coordinate.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our equation:\n\n$$\nz=4r\n$$\n\nThis equation requires that the distance from any point on the surface to the $xy$-plane equals $4$ times the distance from that point to the $z$-axis. In other words, the tangent of the angle between any position vector on the surface and the $z$-axis must be equal to\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{r}{z} = \\dfrac{r}{4r} = \\dfrac14.\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, $\\theta$ can vary arbitrarily in its domain. \n\nTherefore, our surface is the half-cone shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038052/assets/69978d3f1026cf83019924c44d66f0e510ce8c84b35d10c77c270b87809b30aa.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186298615 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "eae7e5be-e883-5658-84a0-5de5da567887" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Polar Equation of a Given Surface"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a8e61caf-56e7-56c4-8575-acc217f67f41" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298617} {:answer/id #uuid "df95055e-53af-5995-9bc4-42f0837eb3cd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r=\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298618} {:answer/id #uuid "9c53534e-c3c1-5869-ad0d-ef87539795d5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\theta =1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298619} {:answer/id #uuid "b93eaa0f-be2f-56b7-b7ba-d85a1d9dc808" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z=r" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298620} {:answer/id #uuid "f97b6bc3-f71a-5f4b-bec7-30d94af277e0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298621}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a8e61caf-56e7-56c4-8575-acc217f67f41" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298617} :answer-field/id #uuid "d19fe1c9-6e46-5129-a611-e04ef5954fdb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298616}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "0065c9b1-05e8-5a12-8df1-79775a8c9154" :question/math-academy-id "q-104204" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038052/assets/bfd160c9286927c9cafdf19fb591eba6b1c1a49ee0362c15c5a2fd26d50cdbc6.png)\n\nWhat is the equation of the surface shown above in cylindrical polar coordinates?" :question/worked-solution "The position of a point in cylindrical polar coordinates is written as $(r,\\theta ,z),$ where \n\n- $r\\ge 0$ and $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi )$ are the usual plane polar coordinates, and\n\n- $z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )$ is the usual Cartesian $z$-coordinate.\n\nThe picture shows a circular cylinder of radius $1$ whose axis of symmetry is the $z$-axis. \n\nWe note the following:\n\n- Since the distance from any point on our surface to the $z$-axis equals $1,$ we must have $r=1$ for every point on the surface.\n\n- On the other hand, $\\theta $ and $z$ can vary arbitrarily in their respective domains.\n\nTherefore, the equation of the surface in cylindrical polar coordinates is $r=1.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186298622 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "eae7e5be-e883-5658-84a0-5de5da567887" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Polar Equation of a Given Surface"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "61f10c8b-2921-5587-9e6f-28b52f2b976d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z=2\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298624} {:answer/id #uuid "7e803a30-30a9-5665-9ad6-597fdeccf261" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\theta =2r" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298625} {:answer/id #uuid "219c9bda-c31c-575b-8c4d-511748675442" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r=2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298626} {:answer/id #uuid "dcf5e303-ad49-57f4-b254-6349f100e658" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z=2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298627} {:answer/id #uuid "3ae916f3-60c4-5573-b0ed-e84e54541b46" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\theta =2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298628}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3ae916f3-60c4-5573-b0ed-e84e54541b46" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\theta =2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298628} :answer-field/id #uuid "331d6402-0225-5295-b92d-5dd91e02906a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answ