z$-coordinate.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our equation:\n\n$$\n\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{2}\n$$\n\nThis equation requires that if we project the vector $\\overset{\\to }{OP}$ onto the $xy$-plane for every point $P$ on our surface, the resulting vector makes an angle of $\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ radians with the positive $x$-axis. So, we must have $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ for every point on the surface. On the other hand, $r$ and $z$ can vary arbitrarily in their respective domains.\n\nTherefore, our surface is a half-plane that passes through the $z$-axis, shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038052/assets/4eefd7f719ccb0c34407d6c02ef1adeb283f45e80ed6a95525661b84e39b3a8d.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186298678 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2f4472e5-68af-5e8c-83cb-ceab2a6dfe1e" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Cones and Half-Cones"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3ac8f046-72df-576f-b21c-21006508677d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A cone" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298680} {:answer/id #uuid "0e5b890b-c961-51ca-b0e7-db193ae6f885" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A half-plane that passes through the $z$-axis" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298681} {:answer/id #uuid "29bdfdcc-e59d-5c17-b89d-3d7243da78b4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A sphere" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298682} {:answer/id #uuid "5994d573-9f3d-548c-945d-3db9786a649e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A plane that is parallel to the $xy$-plane" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298683} {:answer/id #uuid "efe2136f-2d64-5094-b77a-5e5d844a1c3b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A circular cylinder" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298684}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3ac8f046-72df-576f-b21c-21006508677d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A cone" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298680} :answer-field/id #uuid "7791552b-c126-59c8-a32e-8b164e58dee3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298679}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f99fad58-2ad5-52cb-8779-f22e2997958e" :question/math-academy-id "q-104236" :question/problem "What surface is defined by the equation ${z}^{2}=4{r}^{2}$ in cylindrical polar coordinates?" :question/worked-solution "The position of a point in cylindrical polar coordinates is written as $(r,\\theta ,z),$ where \n\n- $r\\ge 0$ and $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi )$ are the usual plane polar coordinates, and\n\n- $z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )$ is the usual Cartesian $z$-coordinate.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our equation:\n\n$$\n{z}^{2}=4{r}^{2}\n$$\n\nThis equation requires that the square of the distance from any point on the surface to the $xy$-plane equals $4$ times the square of the distance from that point to the $z$-axis. In other words, the tangent of the angle between any position vector on the surface and the $z$-axis must be equal to\n\n$$\n\\frac{r}{z}=\\sqrt{\\frac{{r}^{2}}{{z}^{2}}}=\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{{r}^{2}}{4{r}^{2}}}=\\pm \\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nSince the tangent of this angle can be a positive or negative number, the $z$-coordinate can also be positive or negative.\n\nOn the other hand, $\\theta $ can vary arbitrarily in its domain. \n\nTherefore, our surface is the cone shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038052/assets/7c8d0cefa5eb8da76f6cba59570670e4078c278c801aeb12344dce3183733149.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186298685 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2f4472e5-68af-5e8c-83cb-ceab2a6dfe1e" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Cones and Half-Cones"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "71344778-774e-5983-9411-00f1674cccad" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A half-plane that passes through the $z$-axis" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298687} {:answer/id #uuid "03eaa07a-3e3b-58a1-a6b4-eb99bb210eea" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A sphere" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298688} {:answer/id #uuid "edf9dcb0-d9fc-58a1-a425-749613cb6369" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A plane that is parallel to the $xy$-plane" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298689} {:answer/id #uuid "f4cab9ef-7313-5f90-97fc-aae7b83957b5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A half-cone" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298690} {:answer/id #uuid "38433e0a-17e0-5b5e-a3b6-f42677e1ee7e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A circular cylinder" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298691}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f4cab9ef-7313-5f90-97fc-aae7b83957b5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A half-cone" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298690} :answer-field/id #uuid "ebb1c09e-c17a-5dbc-98ad-a9fa87e9637d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298686}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "0d1330ed-ad27-5e66-8808-a6a63e5c430b" :question/math-academy-id "q-104241" :question/problem "What surface is defined by the equation $z=\\frac{1}{5}r$ in cylindrical polar coordinates?" :question/worked-solution "The position of a point in cylindrical polar coordinates is written as $(r,\\theta ,z),$ where \n\n- $r\\ge 0$ and $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi )$ are the usual plane polar coordinates, and\n\n- $z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )$ is the usual Cartesian $z$-coordinate.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our equation:\n\n$$\nz=\\frac{1}{5}r\n$$\n\nThis equation requires that the distance from any point on the surface to the $xy$-plane equals one-fifth the distance from that point to the $z$-axis. In other words, the tangent of the angle between any position vector on the surface and the $z$-axis must be equal to\n\n$$\n\\frac{r}{z}=\\frac{r}{(\\frac{1}{5}r)}=5.\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, $\\theta $ can vary arbitrarily in its domain. \n\nTherefore, our surface is the half-cone shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038052/assets/69978d3f1026cf83019924c44d66f0e510ce8c84b35d10c77c270b87809b30aa.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186298692 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2f4472e5-68af-5e8c-83cb-ceab2a6dfe1e" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Cones and Half-Cones"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "76093b81-ef74-5a2f-9f49-6725ef31434b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z=\\pi r" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298694} {:answer/id #uuid "dcb27d5a-d9c2-5167-bfaa-321f4e3de7a7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z=\\frac{r}{\\pi }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298695} {:answer/id #uuid "7aa91e70-d925-56c0-96e4-a832d5c83bdb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{z}^{2}=\\frac{{r}^{2}}{\\pi }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298696} {:answer/id #uuid "ab77c22f-f720-54d6-b3e3-3a2764fe22dd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z=r" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298697} {:answer/id #uuid "20bd8848-9e0c-5d09-96f1-ac15185f1393" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{z}^{2}=4{r}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298698}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ab77c22f-f720-54d6-b3e3-3a2764fe22dd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z=r" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298697} :answer-field/id #uuid "820d1dd5-a82c-5083-8f31-61009dce45b9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298693}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "aaf763ca-bf8e-52b8-944d-3a49a19773cf" :question/math-academy-id "q-104243" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038052/assets/446989d58605132a7e8e361b12127341c490845d893a792662b41c3d086e71e6.png)\n\nWhat is the equation of the surface shown above in cylindrical polar coordinates?" :question/worked-solution "The position of a point in cylindrical polar coordinates is written as $(r,\\theta ,z),$ where \n\n- $r\\ge 0$ and $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi )$ are the usual plane polar coordinates, and\n\n- $z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )$ is the usual Cartesian $z$-coordinate.\n\nThe picture shows a circular half-cone whose axis of symmetry is the $z$-axis. \n\nWe note the following:\n\n- From the diagram, the angle between the position vector of any point on the surface and the $z$-axis must be equal $\\frac{\\pi }{4}.$ \n\nThe tangent of this angle equals the ratio between the distance $r$ from the point to the $z$-axis and the distance $z$ from the point to the $xy$-plane. Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{r}{z} & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\ \\frac{r}{z} & =1 \\\\ z & =r.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- On the other hand, $\\theta $ can vary arbitrarily in its domain.\n\nTherefore, the equation of the surface in cylindrical polar coordinates is $z=r.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186298699 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2f4472e5-68af-5e8c-83cb-ceab2a6dfe1e" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Cones and Half-Cones"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bdd1a213-ca44-5f8e-a993-c39bb868c2b7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z=\\sqrt{2}r" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298701} {:answer/id #uuid "8b894cf3-2c3c-5bd9-940e-2a2c403d913c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{z}^{2}={r}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298702} {:answer/id #uuid "5909e768-6da1-54d5-aa8f-fae130ce052d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{z}^{2}=2{r}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298703} {:answer/id #uuid "2fe9d632-9f48-5ffd-8392-5752b5ab1536" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{z}^{2}=\\sqrt{2}{r}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298704} {:answer/id #uuid "004b9419-b6b0-5a28-b27a-cd0883308785" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z=2r" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298705}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8b894cf3-2c3c-5bd9-940e-2a2c403d913c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{z}^{2}={r}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298702} :answer-field/id #uuid "f388930e-6b64-5ca7-b586-752b4fbae6e7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298700}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "d8134d34-0cf4-5c60-943e-99085b23f341" :question/math-academy-id "q-104329" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038052/assets/a89469b49ecb4f40ba8378e1ce5404f5a1cf0eb4e78922529d9dce368b28c82d.png)\n\nWhat is the equation of the surface shown above in cylindrical polar coordinates?" :question/worked-solution "The position of a point in cylindrical polar coordinates is written as $(r,\\theta ,z),$ where \n\n- $r\\ge 0$ and $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi )$ are the usual plane polar coordinates, and\n\n- $z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )$ is the usual Cartesian $z$-coordinate.\n\nThe picture shows a circular cone whose axis of symmetry is the $z$-axis. \n\nWe note the following:\n\n- From the diagram, the angle between the position vector of any point on the surface and the $z$-axis must be equal $\\frac{\\pi }{4}.$ \n\nThe tangent of this angle equals the ratio between the distance $r$ from the point to the $z$-axis and the distance $z$ from the point to the $xy$-plane. Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{r}{z} & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\ \\frac{r}{z} & =1 \\\\ z & =r.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- On the other hand, $\\theta $ can vary arbitrarily in its domain.\n\nFinally, recall that $r\\ge 0.$ Since we wish to include both half-cones, we need to include $z=-r$ as well. We can combine the two equations by squaring both sides.\n\nTherefore, the equation of the surface in cylindrical polar coordinates is ${z}^{2}={r}^{2}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186298706 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2f4472e5-68af-5e8c-83cb-ceab2a6dfe1e" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Cones and Half-Cones"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d24832bb-e71f-5d38-80e1-98dc8faa06ce" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A half-plane that passes through the $z$-axis" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298708} {:answer/id #uuid "f348914f-c5b7-5caf-a744-3d802555f23f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A sphere" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298709} {:answer/id #uuid "9579dada-b4e4-5c3f-b556-19b1ffeba3fd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A plane that is parallel to the $xy$-plane" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298710} {:answer/id #uuid "8f436fd7-f020-5c3c-8d5a-7fd4ddd0ac21" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A half-cone" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298711} {:answer/id #uuid "2b8002a2-cb37-5486-a719-afc7e46f795f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A circular cylinder" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298712}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8f436fd7-f020-5c3c-8d5a-7fd4ddd0ac21" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A half-cone" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298711} :answer-field/id #uuid "44c15826-52c1-537f-8434-1ee67c4f0ec9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298707}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c5c71394-0fe1-554d-95f3-37229cba692d" :question/math-academy-id "q-104335" :question/problem "What surface is defined by the equation $z=3r$ in cylindrical polar coordinates?" :question/worked-solution "The position of a point in cylindrical polar coordinates is written as $(r,\\theta ,z),$ where \n\n- $r\\ge 0$ and $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi )$ are the usual plane polar coordinates, and\n\n- $z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )$ is the usual Cartesian $z$-coordinate.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our equation:\n\n$$\nz=3r\n$$\n\nThis equation requires that the distance from any point on the surface to the $xy$-plane equals $3$ times the distance from that point to the $z$-axis. In other words, the tangent of the angle between any position vector on the surface and the $z$-axis must be equal to\n\n$$\n\\frac{r}{z}=\\frac{r}{3r}=\\frac{1}{3}.\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, $\\theta $ can vary arbitrarily in its domain. \n\nTherefore, our surface is the half-cone shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038052/assets/69978d3f1026cf83019924c44d66f0e510ce8c84b35d10c77c270b87809b30aa.png)"}]}
